From: freeforall1932-design <300004558+freeforall1932-design@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 04 Oct 2026 09:10:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] P1-42 / U-72: latch the cancel flag — consume it in
 onProcessFinished, never clear it early

Finding U-72 (F:NF-15, fix-order P1-42, state OPEN at main fe4f0a7):

    cancelling_ cleared after 3s wait → spurious failure dialog after
    Cancelled — cancelRun() resets the flag right after
    waitForFinished(3000); if the engine dies later, finished() arrives with
    cancelling_==false and shows the failure QMessageBox.

cancelRun() armed cancelling_, killed the engine, waited up to 3 s, then
cleared the flag unconditionally. Two delivery shapes race that clear: a
waitForFinished() TIMEOUT (engine still dying), and a finished() signal
that is queued but not yet dispatched even when the wait succeeded. Either
way the late finished() found cancelling_ == false, took the failure
branch, and popped "Optimization failed (exit …)" immediately after the
status line had just said "Cancelled." — the UI contradicting itself in
one breath. (The N-11 "Run had already finished" guard covers a different
gap: an exit-0 that raced the cancel. It does not cover this crash-exit
path.)

Fix — the flag becomes a LATCH with exactly one owner:

  * cancelRun() arms it ONLY when a process was actually running (so it can
    never outlive the killed run's own notification) and no longer clears
    it after the wait;
  * onProcessFinished() reads-and-consumes it FIRST, before the verdict
    branch — one completion, one consumption. The failure branch then tests
    the consumed local.

The consume-before-verdict placement is load-bearing: a naive "just keep
the flag" repair lets it survive past this finished() and silently swallow
the NEXT run's genuine failure — a new pit of exactly this finding's class
(COMPILED_AUDIT §8). The killPreview() preview path is deliberately
untouched: per-run QProcess + seq guard already prevents the stale-read,
so there is no second flag to mis-consume (this is the pattern the fix
aligns with).

Proof plan (Qt-class row — CI proves what this sandbox cannot):

  * cmake -S . -B build-cmake -DBUILD_GUI=ON && cmake --build build-cmake:
    must compile clean (the offscreen harness CI step does this on linux +
    windows).
  * QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen ./build-cmake/test_gui_offscreen: the
    existing T4/T9/T21 cancel-and-partial cases must stay green (no
    behavioural change on the happy path or the already-finished path).
  * New harness case T22 (sketch in the accompanying audit MD): fake-engine
    run, Cancel, artificial delivery of the killed run's finished() AFTER
    cancelRun() returns — assert no "Optimization failed" dialog appears
    and the status stays "Cancelled.". RED on the old code (dialog shows),
    green here.
  * Sentinel (CI): grep that cancelRun() no longer contains the bare
    trailing `cancelling_ = false;` after the wait block.

After green CI proof: §5 row U-72 -> FIXED (proof: harness: T22 (offscreen)),
PAIR rows P1-42 -> DONE with U-70 once its sibling patch lands, then
check_docs.sh --emit + G10 base re-anchor. Note for the register: U-12
(P1-24, the full async state machine) stays OPEN — this patch removes the
race, not the blocking waits themselves.
---
 .../gifscythe/src/qtui/MainWindow.cpp         | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/working_code/gifscythe/src/qtui/MainWindow.cpp b/working_code/gifscythe/src/qtui/MainWindow.cpp
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/working_code/gifscythe/src/qtui/MainWindow.cpp
+++ b/working_code/gifscythe/src/qtui/MainWindow.cpp
@@ -503,12 +503,22 @@ void MainWindow::cancelRun() {
   // kill() makes gifsicle exit with a non-zero/crash status, which normally
   // routes through the "optimization failed" branch. Mark the cancellation so
   // onProcessFinished doesn't surface a spurious error dialog mid-cancel.
-  cancelling_ = true;
-  if (process_ && process_->state() != QProcess::NotRunning) {
+  // U-72 / P1-42: the flag is LATCHED — arming and clearing live in two
+  // different places now. The old code cleared it right after
+  // waitForFinished(3000), but that wait can time out, and even when it
+  // succeeds the finished() signal can be queued-but-undelivered; a
+  // finished() that arrived after the clear took the failure branch and
+  // popped a spurious "Optimization failed" QMessageBox immediately after
+  // "Cancelled.". The latch is armed only when a process was ACTUALLY
+  // running (it can never outlive the killed run's own notification) and is
+  // consumed once by onProcessFinished — see there.
+  const bool engineWasRunning =
+      (process_ && process_->state() != QProcess::NotRunning);
+  if (engineWasRunning) {
+    cancelling_ = true;
     process_->kill();
     process_->waitForFinished(3000);
   }
-  cancelling_ = false;
 
   // N-11: the engine may have already exited successfully in the gap between
   // the last event-loop turn and this call. pendingPartial_ then holds VERIFIED
@@ -548,8 +558,16 @@ void MainWindow::onProcessFinished(int exitCode, QProcess::ExitStatus status) {
   // Drain stdout so it doesn't fill the pipe (we don't use it when -o is set).
   process_->readAllStandardOutput();
 
+  // U-72 / P1-42: read-and-consume the cancel latch for THIS completion,
+  // whatever the verdict turns out to be. Simply KEEPING the flag (the naive
+  // fix for the early clear) would let it outlive one finished() and
+  // silently swallow the NEXT run's genuine failure — a new pit of exactly
+  // the class this finding belongs to. One completion, one consumption.
+  const bool wasCancelling = cancelling_;
+  cancelling_ = false;
+
   if (status != QProcess::NormalExit || exitCode != 0) {
-    if (cancelling_) {
+    if (wasCancelling) {
       // A user-initiated cancel (or window close) kills the engine, which then
       // reports a non-zero exit. That's expected, not a failure to alarm about;
       // cancelRun() finishes the cleanup and sets the "Cancelled." status.
